## Database

MongoDB via `@nestjs/mongoose`. Requires Node.js >=20.19.0 (Mongoose 9). Set `MONGO_URI` in `.env` (defaults to a local instance on 27017). Registered in `AppModule` via `MongooseModule.forRootAsync(...)` (async so tests can override `MONGO_URI` before the connection is opened); feature modules register their own schemas with `MongooseModule.forFeature(...)`. `GET /healthz` reports live connectivity; `app.enableShutdownHooks()` (in `main.ts`) makes Nest close the connection automatically on SIGTERM/SIGINT, no extra wiring needed. Note: `mongodb-memory-server` is included so tests can run against a real in-memory MongoDB without a local install, whether or not you also picked the testing module.

### Transactions

Multi-document transactions require MongoDB to be running as a replica set (even a single-node one) — a plain standalone MongoDB instance doesn't support them at all. Mongoose's default retryable-writes behavior means the error you'll actually see is usually `This MongoDB deployment does not support retryable writes` (older driver/server combinations instead throw `Transaction numbers are only allowed on a replica set member or mongos`). Inject the connection and manage the session yourself where you need one:

```ts
constructor(@InjectConnection() private readonly connection: Connection) {}

async placeOrder(dto: PlaceOrderDto) {
  const session = await this.connection.startSession();
  try {
    let order;
    await session.withTransaction(async () => {
      [order] = await this.orderModel.create([{ ...dto }], { session });
      await this.inventoryModel.updateOne({ _id: dto.itemId }, { $inc: { stock: -1 } }, { session });
    });
    return order;
  } finally {
    await session.endSession();
  }
}
```

The default `test/setup.ts` here starts a standalone `MongoMemoryServer`, which also doesn't support transactions — swap in `MongoMemoryReplSet` from the same package if you need to test transactional code.
